  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    64,
    16,
    3072
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A PlayStation 2 is the biggest machine in the bundle, and most of the mmap arena is not the console: 285MB is the machine (32MB of EE RAM, the bios and rom areas, the IOP's 2MB, the vector units, and the vtlb's two page tables over a 4GB address space) and 305MB is the recompiler cache region this build reserves and never writes. The sandbox charges for pages that are TOUCHED, so most of that is address space rather than memory - but the arena has to be big enough to hold it. The mmap arena is the one that matters and the one that grew: musl sends every LARGE allocation to mmap rather than to the heap, so the machine's memory, the code region and - when the renderer setting asks for OpenGL - a whole Mesa all come out of it. Growing sbrk instead does nothing, and taking from mmap to pay for it makes startup fail earlier, which is how this was found. The whole layout has to fit inside a single 4 GiB region.",
  "video": {
    "greenzoneSurvivesReload": true,
    "_comment_greenzone": "TRUE: a project's greenzone this core drew on a GPU may be loaded by a later session (docs/gpu-bridge.md in Chimera, 'Keeping the greenzone across sessions'). Measured 2026-09-16: Gran Turismo 4, 1000 frames, the greenzone (158.7 MB) written at frame 900 and loaded in a fresh process; frame 999 identical (0 of 286,720 pixels differ, 1,066 colours), a control frame (200) differing by 99.51%, no guest death. Until 2026-10-18 this was PCSX2's name on a list in Chimera.",
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded.",
    "drawEveryFrame": true,
    "_comment_drawEveryFrame": "Never tell this core to stop drawing; turbo skips only the readback. Turbo's bet is that a skipped frame is work deferred - but this renderer's picture lives on the far side of the GPU bridge and PERSISTS there, so a screen the game paints once and then leaves alone is painted by exactly one frame, and skipping that frame loses it for good. A warm-up cannot fix that: measured on Gran Turismo 4, drawing the last ten frames before a seek's destination still leaves 1.3% to 2.8% of the picture wrong at every point tried, while drawing throughout is exact. It costs almost nothing - 1500 frames on a GTX 1060: 8.28s turbo, 8.30s drawing, 9.75s drawing AND reading back - because what turbo was really saving is the readback, which this still skips. Both renderers need it: this is the display stage, not the GPU.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the display circuits the game programs. 640x448 is the ordinary NTSC frame; 2560x2048 holds one at 4x internal resolution and a 1080i frame at 1x. A bigger frame is shrunk to fit.",
    "width": 2560,
    "height": 2048,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 448,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "SPU2 mixes at 48kHz on a PlayStation 2 and a System 246, so a frame is about 800 stereo samples. The two faster boards state their own rate (a machine's audioRate): about 1070 and 1200 samples a frame. The time stretcher upstream turns on by default is OFF here: a frame's samples are the ones the chip produced, and buffering is the frontend's business.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".iso": "PS2",
    ".bin": "PS2",
    ".img": "PS2",
    ".chd": "PS2",
    ".cso": "PS2",
    ".zso": "PS2",
    ".gz": "PS2",
    ".elf": "PS2"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "PlayStation 2 Controllers",
    "_comment": "EIGHT slots: two physical ports, each of which a multitap turns into four. P1 and P2 ARE the two ports; P3 to P5 are port 1's multitap slots B to D and P6 to P8 are port 2's, which is PCSX2's own unified-slot order and the order a game sees. Every slot takes a DualShock 2. The two physical ports also take the instruments - a guitar, a Jogcon, a Negcon, a Pop'n controller - and Namco's GunCon 2 light gun, and carry the extra controls those need; a multitap slot does not, because nobody builds that machine and every declared control costs a column in every movie ever recorded with this core. The controls of a device a port is not set to are simply never read. The GUN is the odd one here: it is a USB device rather than a controller, so setting a port to guncon2 puts a gun in the USB socket of that number and leaves the controller socket empty - one setting, because it is one player. Its Gun X and Gun Y are an ABSOLUTE position on the screen (-32768 the left or top edge, 32767 the right or bottom one) rather than a stick's displacement, and Gun Offscreen is the shot taken with the gun pointed away from the screen, which every light-gun game reads as a reload. After the eight slots, the console's own controls: Previous Disc and Next Disc move a selector over the project's discs and touch nothing; Disc Swap is the tray - open for as long as it is held, and closing on the selected disc (or, with the selector untouched, on the next one). The selector is there when the project has more than one disc, the tray when the machine started from a disc.",
    "buttons": [
      "P1 Up",
      "P1 Down",
      "P1 Left",
      "P1 Right",
      "P1 Start",
      "P1 Select",
      "P1 Square",
      "P1 Cross",
      "P1 Circle",
      "P1 Triangle",
      "P1 L1",
      "P1 R1",
      "P1 L2",
      "P1 R2",
      "P1 L3",
      "P1 R3",
      "P1 Analog",
      "P1 Negcon A",
      "P1 Negcon B",
      "P1 Negcon I",
      "P1 Negcon II",
      "P1 Strum Up",
      "P1 Strum Down",
